1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,283
August eighteenth, fifteen ninety.

2
00:00:02,503 --> 00:00:07,147
A man named John White walks into a colony he left three years earlier.

3
00:00:07,647 --> 00:00:08,463
It's empty.

4
00:00:08,683 --> 00:00:10,329
Over a hundred people, gone.

5
00:00:10,549 --> 00:00:11,932
Houses taken apart.

6
00:00:12,152 --> 00:00:13,079
No bodies.

7
00:00:13,299 --> 00:00:14,227
No graves.

8
00:00:14,727 --> 00:00:18,228
And carved into a wooden post at the entrance, one word.

9
00:00:18,448 --> 00:00:19,272
Croatoan.

10
00:00:20,072 --> 00:00:21,207
Not a great note.

11
00:00:21,427 --> 00:00:23,736
I've seen better messages on a fridge.

12
00:00:25,136 --> 00:00:25,665
Hi.

13
00:00:25,885 --> 00:00:32,579
I'm Archie, this is The Quiet Archive, and today we're opening the file on the Lost Colony of Roanoke.

14
00:00:33,079 --> 00:00:36,341
The oldest unsolved mystery in American history.

15
00:00:36,561 --> 00:00:38,255
Older than the United States.

16
00:00:38,475 --> 00:00:41,661
Older than, honestly, most of the furniture in here.

17
00:00:42,161 --> 00:00:47,359
So who were these people, why did their leader leave them, and what did that one word mean?

18
00:00:47,579 --> 00:00:48,931
Let's go to the records.

19
00:00:50,331 --> 00:00:51,690
First, some context.

20
00:00:51,910 --> 00:00:54,152
Roanoke was not England's first try.

21
00:00:54,372 --> 00:00:55,406
It was its second.

22
00:00:55,906 --> 00:01:05,727
In fifteen eighty five, a group of soldiers led by Ralph Lane built an outpost on Roanoke Island, off the coast of what's now North Carolina.

23
00:01:06,227 --> 00:01:07,417
It did not go well.

24
00:01:07,637 --> 00:01:11,862
They ran low on food, and they made enemies of the local tribes.

25
00:01:12,362 --> 00:01:17,446
So when Sir Francis Drake sailed by the next year, they basically hitched a ride home.

26
00:01:17,946 --> 00:01:24,276
A relief fleet showed up two weeks later, found the place empty, and left fifteen men behind to hold it.

27
00:01:24,496 --> 00:01:26,559
They were attacked, and never seen again.

28
00:01:27,359 --> 00:01:28,155
So yes.

29
00:01:28,375 --> 00:01:32,469
Roanoke had a disappearance problem before the famous disappearance.

30
00:01:33,869 --> 00:01:36,563
In fifteen eighty seven, England tried again.

31
00:01:36,783 --> 00:01:38,227
This time with families.

32
00:01:38,727 --> 00:01:40,957
About a hundred and fifteen colonists.

33
00:01:41,177 --> 00:01:42,615
Men, women, and children.

34
00:01:42,835 --> 00:01:45,516
Their governor was an artist named John White.

35
00:01:46,016 --> 00:01:47,891
The plan was not even Roanoke.

36
00:01:48,111 --> 00:01:51,696
They were supposed to settle further north, in Chesapeake Bay.

37
00:01:53,096 --> 00:02:01,158
But the ship's pilot, a man named Simon Fernandes, dropped them off at Roanoke instead, and refused to take them further.

38
00:02:01,658 --> 00:02:06,946
Imagine booking a flight to New York and the pilot just goes, eh, Delaware's fine.

39
00:02:07,166 --> 00:02:07,875
Get out.

40
00:02:08,375 --> 00:02:13,443
So the colonists settled into the old buildings on Roanoke Island and got to work.

41
00:02:14,843 --> 00:02:20,772
On August eighteenth, fifteen eighty seven, White's daughter Eleanor gave birth to a baby girl.

42
00:02:20,992 --> 00:02:22,077
Virginia Dare.

43
00:02:22,577 --> 00:02:25,400
The first English child born in the Americas.

44
00:02:25,620 --> 00:02:27,721
John White was now a grandfather.

45
00:02:28,221 --> 00:02:30,192
Then the colonists made a request.

46
00:02:30,412 --> 00:02:31,662
Supplies were short.

47
00:02:31,882 --> 00:02:35,179
They needed someone to sail back to England and bring help.

48
00:02:35,679 --> 00:02:37,057
And they chose the governor.

49
00:02:37,277 --> 00:02:38,143
John White.

50
00:02:38,643 --> 00:02:39,803
He agreed to go.

51
00:02:40,023 --> 00:02:46,279
Nine days after his granddaughter was born, he sailed for England, planning to be back within a year.

52
00:02:47,679 --> 00:02:49,603
He made it to England in November.

53
00:02:49,823 --> 00:02:50,452
Great.

54
00:02:50,672 --> 00:02:52,240
Now he just needed a ship.

55
00:02:52,740 --> 00:02:53,432
Problem.

56
00:02:53,652 --> 00:02:55,354
Spain was about to invade.

57
00:02:55,854 --> 00:03:02,797
The Spanish Armada was coming, and Queen Elizabeth ordered that every useful ship stay home to defend England.

58
00:03:03,297 --> 00:03:06,273
Which is a very reasonable decision for a country.

59
00:03:06,493 --> 00:03:08,502
And a terrible one for a grandfather.

60
00:03:09,902 --> 00:03:13,334
White did manage to get two small ships in fifteen eighty eight.

61
00:03:13,554 --> 00:03:18,232
They were attacked by French sailors on the way, robbed, and forced to turn back.

62
00:03:18,732 --> 00:03:20,651
Even the backup plan got robbed.

63
00:03:21,151 --> 00:03:24,808
England beat the Armada, but the ban on ships kept going.

64
00:03:25,028 --> 00:03:29,922
It took White until fifteen ninety to finally get back across the Atlantic.

65
00:03:30,422 --> 00:03:31,958
Three years after he left.

66
00:03:33,358 --> 00:03:36,966
He landed on Roanoke on August eighteenth, fifteen ninety.

67
00:03:37,186 --> 00:03:38,900
His granddaughter's third birthday.

68
00:03:39,400 --> 00:03:40,885
The settlement was empty.

69
00:03:41,105 --> 00:03:43,048
The houses had been taken down.

70
00:03:43,268 --> 00:03:47,334
A palisade, a wall of wooden stakes, had been built around the site.

71
00:03:47,834 --> 00:03:50,460
Several chests had been buried and dug up again.

72
00:03:50,680 --> 00:03:52,226
Three of them were White's own.

73
00:03:52,446 --> 00:03:55,736
His books, his maps, his paintings, ruined by the weather.

74
00:03:56,236 --> 00:03:57,609
But there were no bodies.

75
00:03:57,829 --> 00:03:58,757
No graves.

76
00:03:58,977 --> 00:04:00,356
No signs of a fight.

77
00:04:01,756 --> 00:04:03,199
And there were two carvings.

78
00:04:03,419 --> 00:04:05,493
The letters C, R, O on a tree.

79
00:04:05,993 --> 00:04:09,376
And the full word, Croatoan, on a post at the entrance.

80
00:04:09,876 --> 00:04:11,883
Here's the part most retellings skip.

81
00:04:12,103 --> 00:04:15,764
Before he left, White had agreed on a code with the colonists.

82
00:04:16,264 --> 00:04:18,858
If they moved, they would carve where they went.

83
00:04:19,078 --> 00:04:23,460
And if they left because they were in danger, they would carve a cross above it.

84
00:04:24,260 --> 00:04:25,362
There was no cross.

85
00:04:25,862 --> 00:04:31,117
So according to their own system, the message basically said: we moved to Croatoan.

86
00:04:31,337 --> 00:04:32,169
We're fine.

87
00:04:33,569 --> 00:04:38,949
Croatoan was an island about fifty miles south, today part of Hatteras Island.

88
00:04:39,169 --> 00:04:43,243
It was home to the Croatoan people, who had been friendly to the English.

89
00:04:43,743 --> 00:04:45,508
So White did the obvious thing.

90
00:04:45,728 --> 00:04:48,268
He got back on his ship to go to Croatoan.

91
00:04:49,668 --> 00:04:50,815
And then a storm hit.

92
00:04:51,035 --> 00:04:52,541
An anchor cable snapped.

93
00:04:52,761 --> 00:04:59,262
The ship was down to its last working anchor, the weather was getting worse, and the crew refused to risk it.

94
00:04:59,762 --> 00:05:01,880
So the ship turned around and headed home.

95
00:05:02,780 --> 00:05:05,209
John White never returned to America.

96
00:05:05,429 --> 00:05:08,191
He never saw his daughter or his granddaughter again.

97
00:05:09,591 --> 00:05:14,935
I want to stop on that for a second, because the mystery part can make us forget the human part.

98
00:05:15,435 --> 00:05:25,504
This was a grandfather who spent three years fighting a war, a ban, and pirates to get back to his family, and then lost them to bad weather fifty miles away.

99
00:05:26,004 --> 00:05:26,678
Okay.

100
00:05:26,898 --> 00:05:28,132
So where did they go?

101
00:05:28,352 --> 00:05:29,680
Let's look at the theories.

102
00:05:31,080 --> 00:05:31,962
Theory one.

103
00:05:32,182 --> 00:05:37,425
They went to Croatoan, exactly like the post said, and joined the Croatoan people.

104
00:05:37,925 --> 00:05:44,078
Over generations, they would have married into the community, and simply stopped being "the English colony".

105
00:05:44,578 --> 00:05:45,499
Theory two.

106
00:05:45,719 --> 00:05:46,648
They split up.

107
00:05:46,868 --> 00:05:51,457
Some went to Croatoan, others moved inland, maybe toward the mainland.

108
00:05:51,957 --> 00:05:52,911
Theory three.

109
00:05:53,131 --> 00:05:59,055
They tried to reach Chesapeake Bay, their original destination, and were killed there years later.

110
00:05:59,275 --> 00:06:07,641
That idea comes from English writers in the sixteen hundreds, and historians are skeptical of how reliable those writers were.

111
00:06:08,141 --> 00:06:09,088
Theory four.

112
00:06:09,308 --> 00:06:12,647
They died at sea, trying to sail home in a small boat.

113
00:06:13,447 --> 00:06:15,815
And theory five, of course, is aliens.

114
00:06:16,035 --> 00:06:18,142
Theory five is always aliens.

115
00:06:18,362 --> 00:06:20,095
We do not do theory five here.

116
00:06:21,495 --> 00:06:26,211
In the nineteen thirties, a man brought a stone to a university in Georgia.

117
00:06:26,431 --> 00:06:32,685
It was carved with a message, supposedly from Eleanor Dare herself, describing the colony's fate.

118
00:06:33,185 --> 00:06:34,576
Then more stones showed up.

119
00:06:34,796 --> 00:06:35,873
Dozens of them.

120
00:06:36,373 --> 00:06:41,382
In nineteen forty one, a magazine investigation concluded they were fakes.

121
00:06:41,602 --> 00:06:49,085
The writing used words and spellings that didn't fit the period, and some of the stones came from a man who was paid by the stone.

122
00:06:49,585 --> 00:06:53,580
Turns out, if you pay people per mystery, you get a lot of mysteries.

123
00:06:54,980 --> 00:06:57,022
So the archaeologists went looking.

124
00:06:57,242 --> 00:07:06,551
On Hatteras Island, the old Croatoan, digs have turned up English objects from the right era, including part of a sword and pieces of a gun.

125
00:07:07,051 --> 00:07:17,530
Another team, on the mainland at a spot called Site X, found English pottery, after spotting a hidden symbol under a patch on one of John White's own maps.

126
00:07:18,030 --> 00:07:24,097
The problem is that both sites could also be explained by other English visitors, or by trade.

127
00:07:24,317 --> 00:07:26,399
Nobody has found the smoking gun.

128
00:07:26,619 --> 00:07:30,084
Literally, they found part of a gun, but not a smoking one.

129
00:07:30,584 --> 00:07:33,998
And a lot of the original island has simply washed away.

130
00:07:34,218 --> 00:07:37,791
Parts of Roanoke's north shore have eroded by hundreds of feet.

131
00:07:39,191 --> 00:07:40,609
So, the honest version.

132
00:07:41,109 --> 00:07:42,047
Documented.

133
00:07:42,267 --> 00:07:47,337
About a hundred and fifteen colonists were left on Roanoke in fifteen eighty seven.

134
00:07:47,557 --> 00:07:49,412
White couldn't return for three years.

135
00:07:49,632 --> 00:07:56,391
He found the site dismantled, with CRO on a tree and Croatoan on a post, and no distress cross.

136
00:07:56,891 --> 00:07:57,621
Legend.

137
00:07:57,841 --> 00:08:00,423
That they vanished into thin air without a clue.

138
00:08:00,643 --> 00:08:01,742
They left a clue.

139
00:08:01,962 --> 00:08:04,489
They left the clearest clue in this entire genre.

140
00:08:04,989 --> 00:08:14,374
What probably happened is that the colonists did what they said they'd do, went to Croatoan, and over time, their story became part of someone else's.

141
00:08:15,774 --> 00:08:16,954
So what do you think?

142
00:08:17,174 --> 00:08:20,890
Croatoan, the mainland, or somewhere nobody has dug yet?

143
00:08:21,390 --> 00:08:22,634
Tell me in the comments.

144
00:08:22,854 --> 00:08:28,355
I'll read them, slowly, with my reading glasses, which are also my regular glasses.

145
00:08:28,855 --> 00:08:32,933
If you like true stories that history never quite finished, subscribe.

146
00:08:33,153 --> 00:08:37,535
I'm Archie, this is The Quiet Archive, and I'll see you in the next file.
